\begin{abstract}
We develop a rigorous topological model of Wheeler's spacetime foam in which the Planck-scale bifurcation of geometry is encoded as a branched quotient space. Given a topological space $X$ (macroscopic spacetime), a \emph{branch locus} $S \subseteq X$, and an index set $\iota$ of \emph{sheets}, the \emph{foam} $\Foam(X,S,\iota)$ is the quotient of $X \times \iota$ (with $\iota$ discrete) by the relation identifying $(x,i)$ with $(y,j)$ exactly when $x = y$ and either $i = j$ or $x \notin S$; the line with two origins is the case $X=\mathbb{R}$, $S=\{0\}$, $|\iota|=2$. We prove a complete separation-axiom characterisation (Hausdorff iff $X$ is Hausdorff and $S$ is open; $T_1$ iff $X$ is; never $R_1$ when a branch point lies outside $\intr S$), an invisibility theorem (for $\intr S = \varnothing$, every continuous Hausdorff-valued map factors uniquely through the macroscopic projection), and a faithful $\Sym(\iota)$ gauge action which is trivial on all such observables. We compute the metric defect exactly, $|S \setminus \intr S|\,(|\iota|^2 - |\iota|)$, exhibiting the obstruction to metrizability as a pure boundary quantity, and prove the covering dichotomy: the projection is a covering map iff $S$ is clopen. On the stochastic side, a Bernoulli$(p)$ branch measure over $N$ Planck cells gives Hausdorff probability $(1-p)^N \le e^{-pN}$, entropy $N\mathcal{H}(p) \le N\log 2$ with equality iff $p=1/2$, an entropy--geometry duality $H_{1/2} = \log(2^{\exc})$, second moment $Np(1-p)+(Np)^2$, and the Chebyshev concentration bound $p(1-p)/(N\varepsilon^2)$ for the branch density. Finally, the scale-halving renormalisation flow on lattice foams is shown to be \emph{persistent}: its only fixed lattice foam is the single-branch-point foam $S=\{0\}$, the tower of any nonzero lattice converges to $\{0\}$ rather than to smooth spacetime, and that limit is still non-Hausdorff with metric defect exactly $2$.
\end{abstract}

